            "63-degree sector; 22 to 26% from the knolls beside it) and, marginally, the Clapier (a "
            "2-degree sliver from the col, 5% from 1 km down, 8% from the ridge) see any plain from the "
            "col or the descent. The Mont-Cenis sees nothing from the col or the first 10 km of its "
            "descent and has a half-percent sliver from three cells of the terrain grid on the ridge "
            "above it; the Petit Mont-Cenis (the second col of the Mont-Cenis plateau), the Montgenèvre, "
            "the Larche, the Petit St Bernard, the Grand St Bernard and the Col Agnel see none.",
            "A recalibration of all fourteen published radiocarbon dates from the Traversette meadow "
            "against IntCal20 (the dates are those printed by Mahaney and colleagues, with one laboratory "
            "number printed twice); a Ward and Wilson test, a check of whether two dates can be "
            "measurements of one age, showing that the two \"bracketing\" dates at 40 cm, which their "
            "authors say bracket the invasion year, are inconsistent with each other (T = 23.4 against a "
            "critical value of 3.84, p = 1.3 × 10⁻⁶) and both exclude 218 BC; the finding that nothing in "
            "the output of the published age model, the statistical ordering of the dates, places the "
            "churned layer in 218 BC (the words \"firmly\" and \">95%\" of Mahaney and colleagues rest on the "
            "agreement index, Amodel = 97.6, a measure of how well the measured dates fit the model's "
            "ordering, read as a probability); the finding that the paper's own table contradicts itself, "
            "since for one date the range after modelling does not overlap the measured range, beside an "
            "agreement index near 100, which requires the two to agree; and the point that at this part "
            "of the calibration curve no radiocarbon date can separate 218 BC from 207 BC, when "
            "Hannibal's brother Hasdrubal took the same road, or from any year between about 380 and 150 "
            "BC. The dating evidence does not establish a disturbance in 218 BC and does not show whose "
            "animals made the layer.",
            "The textual point that \"Isère\" stands in no manuscript of Polybius (whose manuscripts read "
            "Σκάρας or Σκώρας) or of Livy (sarar, bisarar or arar) and is Cluverius's 1624 conjecture in "
            "both, and that Livy's \"1,000 feet\" for the broken ledge is a Renaissance restoration of a "
            "corrupt line.",
            "The demonstration that Polybius's 1,400 stades to the ascent and 1,200 for the crossing "
            "cannot both be satisfied by any line over the real terrain, so that neither total can be "
            "used against a candidate; and the observation that 1,200 stades is fifteen days at the 80 "
            "stades a day of the stage before the ascent (800 stades in ten days).",
            "An explicit evidence model (14 items, 8 candidates, likelihood ranges argued item by item) "
            "with a shared-source correction (the halving of the nine items that come from one author), "
            "Monte-Carlo bands, a leave-one-out test (each item dropped in turn) and seven named "
            "sceptical scenarios, so that the disagreement between the Clapier and the Traversette can be "
            "traced to three cells of the table: the Island, the Allobrogian ambush and the "
            "practicability judgement.",
            "Two smaller points: the morning setting of the Pleiades computed for 218 BC (true setting 29 "
            "October; first visible setting 4 to 9 November, depending on the visibility threshold "
            "assumed) with two independent astronomy programs and a check against the calendar of "
            "Columella, a writer on agriculture of the first century AD; and Pliny's geography (3.34 and "
            "3.36) used to show that an Island in the Valence plain belongs to one of two peoples of his "
            "list, the Segovellauni or the Cavares, with the Allobroges beyond the Isère, which matches "
            "Polybius's distinction between the Island's people and the Allobroges.",
